   "title": "Fire Behavior",
   "midterm": "34–45",
   "reviewed": "2026-11-03",
   "body": "\n## Overview\n\nThis chapter covers fire chemistry, heat and energy, combustion, fire development, heat transfer, smoke behavior, flammable limits, fire classifications, and dangerous fire conditions.\n\n## Quick notes\n\n### Fire Science Basics\n\n- Matter: Anything with mass and volume.\n- Potential energy: Stored energy.\n- Kinetic energy: Energy of motion.\n- Oxidation: Reaction involving oxygen.\n- Combustion: Rapid oxidation producing heat and usually light.\n- Pyrolysis: Decomposition of material by heat.\n- Autoignition: Ignition caused by heat without an external spark or flame.\n\n### Fire Triangle\n\n- Heat\n- Fuel\n- Oxygen\n\n### Fire Tetrahedron\n\n- Heat\n- Fuel\n- Oxygen\n- Chemical chain reaction\n\n### Three Methods of Heat Transfer\n\n- Conduction: Heat transfer through contact.\n- Convection: Heat transfer through moving gases or liquids.\n- Radiation: Heat transfer through electromagnetic energy.\n\n### Four Stages of Fire Development\n\n1. Incipient: Early fire, localized to the initial fuel.\n2. Growth: Heat release and fire spread increase.\n3. Fully developed: Peak heat release rate in the compartment.\n4. Decay: Fire decreases due to reduced available fuel or oxygen.\n\n### Dangerous Fire Conditions\n\n- Flashover: Rapid transition to widespread compartment involvement.\n- Rollover: Ignition of accumulated combustible gases in the ceiling layer.\n- Backdraft: Explosive ignition associated with air entering an oxygen-deficient fire compartment.\n- Smoke explosion: Ignition of accumulated smoke in a separate area.\n\n### Smoke Behavior\n\n- Plume: Rising column of hot gases, flames, and smoke.\n- Heat stratification: Gases forming layers according to temperature.\n- Flow path: Movement of heat and smoke between areas of differing pressure.\n- Neutral plane: Interface between outgoing hot gases and incoming cooler air at an opening.\n- Laminar smoke flow: Smooth movement.\n- Turbulent smoke flow: Agitated movement.\n- Ventilation-limited fire: Fire growth limited by available oxygen.\n- Fuel-limited fire: Fire growth limited by fuel characteristics or availability.\n\n### Temperatures and Limits\n\n- Flash point: Temperature where enough vapor is produced for momentary ignition.\n- Fire point: Temperature supporting sustained combustion.\n- Boiling point: Temperature where liquid vapor pressure equals surrounding pressure.\n- Ignition temperature: Minimum temperature required for ignition under specified conditions.\n- LEL: Lower Explosive Limit.\n- UEL: Upper Explosive Limit.\n- Flammable range: Concentrations between LEL and UEL.\n\n### Five Fire Classes\n\n- A: Ordinary combustible materials.\n- B: Flammable liquids and gases.\n- C: Energized electrical equipment.\n- D: Combustible metals.\n- K: Cooking oils and fats.\n\n### Toxic Combustion Products\n\n- Carbon monoxide: Toxic gas associated with incomplete combustion.\n- Hydrogen cyanide: Highly toxic gas produced by combustion of certain materials.\n- Smoke can contain hazardous gases and particulates.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Matter** — Anything with mass and volume.\n- **Potential energy** — Stored energy.\n- **Kinetic energy** — Energy of motion.\n- **Oxidation** — Reaction involving oxygen.\n- **Combustion** — Rapid oxidation producing heat and usually light.\n- **Pyrolysis** — Decomposition of material by heat.\n- **Autoignition** — Ignition caused by heat without an external spark or flame.\n- **Fire Triangle** — Heat, Fuel, Oxygen.\n- **Fire Tetrahedron** — Heat, Fuel, Oxygen, Chemical chain reaction.\n- **Conduction** — Heat transfer through contact.\n- **Convection** — Heat transfer through moving gases or liquids.\n- **Radiation** — Heat transfer through electromagnetic energy.\n- **Incipient** — Early fire, localized to the initial fuel.\n- **Growth** — Heat release and fire spread increase.\n- **Fully developed** — Peak heat release rate in the compartment.\n- **Decay** — Fire decreases due to reduced available fuel or oxygen.\n- **Flashover** — Rapid transition to widespread compartment involvement.\n- **Rollover** — Ignition of accumulated combustible gases in the ceiling layer.\n- **Backdraft** — Explosive ignition associated with air entering an oxygen-deficient fire compartment.\n- **Smoke explosion** — Ignition of accumulated smoke in a separate area.\n- **Plume** — Rising column of hot gases, flames, and smoke.\n- **Heat stratification** — Gases forming layers according to temperature.\n- **Flow path** — Movement of heat and smoke between areas of differing pressure.\n- **Neutral plane** — Interface between outgoing hot gases and incoming cooler air at an opening.\n- **Laminar smoke flow** — Smooth movement.\n- **Turbulent smoke flow** — Agitated movement.\n- **Ventilation-limited fire** — Fire growth limited by available oxygen.\n- **Fuel-limited fire** — Fire growth limited by fuel characteristics or availability.\n- **Flash point** — Temperature where enough vapor is produced for momentary ignition.\n- **Fire point** — Temperature supporting sustained combustion.\n- **Boiling point** — Temperature where liquid vapor pressure equals surrounding pressure.\n- **Ignition temperature** — Minimum temperature required for ignition under specified conditions.\n- **LEL** — Lower Explosive Limit.\n- **UEL** — Upper Explosive Limit.\n- **Flammable range** — Concentrations between LEL and UEL.\n- **Carbon monoxide** — Toxic gas associated with incomplete combustion.\n- **Hydrogen cyanide** — Highly toxic gas produced by combustion of certain materials.\n\n## Exam takeaways\n\nUnderstand how fires ignite, grow, transfer heat, move smoke, and create dangerous conditions.\n\n## Sources\n\n- Firefighter_I_Collected_Chapters.docx, Chapter 5\n- Chapter 5 Key Terms Screenshots\n- Chapter outline and starter notes supplied by the student's class brief, 2026-11-03.\n"
