   "midterm": "61–69",
   "reviewed": "2026-11-03",
   "body": "\n## Overview\n\nThis chapter covers rope materials, rope construction, life safety rope, utility rope, knots and hitches, hoisting equipment, and rope care and inspection.\n\n## Quick notes\n\n- General-use life safety rope: Used for appropriate rescue operations.\n- Utility rope: Used for tools and equipment, not life safety.\n- Static rope: Lower elasticity.\n- Dynamic rope: Greater elasticity.\n- Kern: Inner rope core.\n- Mantle: Protective outer sheath.\n- Working end: Rope end used to form knots.\n- Standing part: Portion of rope not being manipulated.\n- Bowline: Creates a fixed loop.\n- Clove hitch: Useful for securing rope around certain tools.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **General-use life safety rope** — Used for appropriate rescue operations.\n- **Utility rope** — Used for tools and equipment, not life safety.\n- **Static rope** — Lower elasticity.\n- **Dynamic rope** — Greater elasticity.\n- **Kern** — Inner rope core.\n- **Mantle** — Protective outer sheath.\n- **Working end** — Rope end used to form knots.\n- **Standing part** — Portion of rope not being manipulated.\n- **Bowline** — Creates a fixed loop.\n- **Clove hitch** — Useful for securing rope around certain tools.\n\n## Exam takeaways\n\nUnderstand rope construction, static versus dynamic rope, knot terminology, and safe rope use.\n\n- Exam Detail: For hoisting equipment with strong, closed handles, such as an exhaust fan, the textbook identifies a bowline with a safety knot.\n\n## Sources\n\n- Firefighter_I_Collected_Chapters.docx, Chapter 8\n- Chapter outline and starter notes supplied by the student's class brief, 2026-11-03.\n"
