   "midterm": "106–114",
   "reviewed": "2026-11-03",
   "body": "\n## Overview\n\nThis chapter will cover door construction, locks and locksets, forcible-entry tools, glass types, irons-set operations, overhead door cutting, and safe entry practices. Full academy procedure review remains pending.\n\n## Quick notes\n\n- Swinging doors are a common pedestrian door type.\n- Mortise lock: Lock assembly fitted into a cavity within a door.\n- Tempered glass: Safety glass commonly used in storefronts and sliding doors.\n- Door frame: Housing surrounding the door opening.\n- Mineral-core door: Type of solid-core fire-resistant door.\n- Irons: Halligan and flathead axe combination.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Mortise lock** — Lock assembly fitted into a cavity within a door.\n- **Tempered glass** — Safety glass commonly used in storefronts and sliding doors.\n- **Door frame** — Housing surrounding the door opening.\n- **Mineral-core door** — Type of solid-core fire-resistant door.\n- **Irons** — Halligan and flathead axe combination.\n\n## Exam takeaways\n\nCommands from the Midterm:\n\n- DRIVE: Continue striking until instructed to stop.\n- WEDGE: Preserve the opening already created.\n\n## Sources\n\n- Hartford County Regional Fire School Firefighter I Midterm, questions 106–114\n- Chapter outline and starter notes supplied by the student's class brief, 2026-11-03.\n"
