   "title": "Establishing and Transferring Command",
   "midterm": "142–150",
   "reviewed": "2026-11-03",
   "body": "\n## Overview\n\nThis chapter covers the Incident Command System, chain of command, incident management, ICS organizational positions, resource assignments, and command transfer.\n\n## Quick notes\n\n- ICS: Incident Command System.\n- NIMS: National Incident Management System.\n- FEMA administers NIMS.\n- Chain of command: Orderly reporting and authority structure.\n- Incident command must be established at every incident.\n- Incident safety officer monitors incident safety.\n- Strike team: Resources of the same kind and type working together under a leader.\n- Task force: Combination of resources assembled for a specific assignment.\n\n### ICS General Staff\n\n1. Operations\n2. Planning\n3. Logistics\n4. Finance/Administration\n\n### Building Sides\n\n- Alpha: Front/designated address side.\n- Bravo: Left.\n- Charlie: Rear.\n- Delta: Right.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **ICS** — Incident Command System.\n- **NIMS** — National Incident Management System.\n- **Chain of command** — Orderly reporting and authority structure.\n- **Strike team** — Resources of the same kind and type working together under a leader.\n- **Task force** — Combination of resources assembled for a specific assignment.\n\n## Exam takeaways\n\nKnow ICS structure, resource groupings, command responsibility, and organizational positions.\n\n## Sources\n\n- Firefighter_I_Collected_Chapters.docx, Chapter 22\n- Chapter outline and starter notes supplied by the student's class brief, 2026-11-03.\n"
