   "number": 29,
   "title": "Hazardous Materials Regulations, Standards, and Laws",
   "reviewed": "2026-11-03",
   "body": "\n## Overview\n\nThis chapter covers hazardous materials response responsibilities, Awareness and Operations training, hazardous materials laws and standards, OSHA requirements, NFPA competencies, the Authority Having Jurisdiction, and responder qualifications.\n\n## Quick notes\n\n- Hazardous materials incidents require careful assessment before action.\n- The material, release conditions, and environment affect the response.\n- Responders must work within their training and assigned responsibilities.\n- Awareness-level personnel focus on recognition, notification, isolation, and initial protective measures within their scope.\n- Operations-level responders may perform trained defensive actions.\n- Mission-specific activities require additional competency and authorization.\n- Hazardous materials technicians have further specialized responsibilities.\n\n### Important Terms\n\n- AHJ: Authority Having Jurisdiction.\n- OSHA: Occupational Safety and Health Administration.\n- HAZWOPER: Hazardous Waste Operations and Emergency Response.\n- NFPA 470: Hazardous materials/WMD response competencies and qualifications in the supplied newer material.\n- EPCRA: Emergency Planning and Community Right-to-Know Act.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **AHJ** — Authority Having Jurisdiction.\n- **OSHA** — Occupational Safety and Health Administration.\n- **HAZWOPER** — Hazardous Waste Operations and Emergency Response.\n- **NFPA 470** — Hazardous materials/WMD response competencies and qualifications in the supplied newer material.\n- **EPCRA** — Emergency Planning and Community Right-to-Know Act.\n\n## Exam takeaways\n\nKnow responder levels, the roles of regulatory agencies, and the limitations of each training level.\n\n## Sources\n\n- Firefighter_I_Collected_Chapters.docx, Chapter 29\n- Chapter outline and starter notes supplied by the student's class brief, 2026-11-03.\n"
